window.docContents[9].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-every-copy-is-induced":{"contents":"A canonical subcube copy preserves Hamming distances: translation is an automorphism,\nand extendEmb d preserves the Hamming weight since the coordinates outside the\nimage of d are all false. So a canonical copy preserves graph distances, i.e. it\nis an isometric embedding.\n\nlemma hammingNorm_extendEmb {n k : ℕ} (d : Fin k ↪ Fin n) (z : Fin k → Bool) :\n    hammingNorm (extendEmb d z) = hammingNorm z := by\n  simp only [hammingNorm]\n  rw [← Finset.card_map d]\n  congr 1\n  ext j\n  by_cases hj : ∃ i, d i = j\n  · obtain ⟨i, rfl⟩ := hj\n    simp\n  · simp_all [extendEmb_apply_of_not_exists _ hj, Bool.zero_eq_false]\n\n\nlemma hammingDist_extendEmb {n k : ℕ} (d : Fin k ↪ Fin n) (x y : Fin k → Bool) :\n    hammingDist (extendEmb d x) (extendEmb d y) = hammingDist x y := by\n  rw [hammingDist_eq_hammingNorm, hammingDist_eq_hammingNorm,\n    BooleanRing.neg_eq, BooleanRing.neg_eq, ← extendEmb_add, hammingNorm_extendEmb]\n\n\nlemma edist_subcubeCopy {n k : ℕ} (b : Fin n → Bool) (d : Fin k ↪ Fin n)\n    (x y : Fin k → Bool) :\n    (hypercubeHamming n).edist (subcubeCopy b d x) (subcubeCopy b d y) =\n      (hypercubeHamming k).edist x y := by\n  have h := hammingDist_aut (hypercubeHamming.translate b) (extendEmb d x)\n    (extendEmb d y)\n  simp only [hypercubeHamming.translate_apply] at h\n  rw [edist_hypercubeHamming, edist_hypercubeHamming, subcubeCopy_apply,\n    subcubeCopy_apply, h, hammingDist_extendEmb]\n\n\nSince adjacency is distance one, a canonical copy reflects adjacency as well as\npreserving it, and by rigidity every copy is canonical. So every copy of Q[k] in\nQ[n] is induced. We prove this first in the Hamming model and then transport it\nto the hypercube along the isomorphisms hypercubeIsoHamming, using Iso.copyCongr.\n\nlemma copy_hypercubeHamming_adj_iff {n k : ℕ}\n    (f : Copy (hypercubeHamming k) (hypercubeHamming n)) {x y : Fin k → Bool} :\n    (hypercubeHamming n).Adj (f x) (f y) ↔ (hypercubeHamming k).Adj x y := by\n  obtain ⟨b, d, rfl⟩ := exists_eq_subcubeCopy f\n  rw [← edist_eq_one_iff_adj, ← edist_eq_one_iff_adj, edist_subcubeCopy]\n\n\nEvery copy of Q[k] in Q[n] is induced./-- Every copy of Q[k] in Q[n] is induced. -/\nlemma copy_hypercube_adj_iff {n k : ℕ} (f : Copy Q[k] Q[n])\n    {x y : HypercubeVert k} : Q[n].Adj (f x) (f y) ↔ Q[k].Adj x y := by\n  have h := copy_hypercubeHamming_adj_iff\n    ((hypercubeIsoHamming k).copyCongr (hypercubeIsoHamming n) f)\n    (x := hypercubeIsoHamming k x) (y := hypercubeIsoHamming k y)\n  simpa [Iso.copyCongr, Iso.map_adj_iff, -hypercubeHamming_adj] using h\n\n\nA copy that reflects adjacency is a graph embedding A ↪g B (Embedding), and\nMathlib writes A ⊴ B (IsIndContained) for the existence of one. So if every copy\nof A in B reflects adjacency, then the copies of A in B and the embeddings\nA ↪g B are the same thing. Mathlib has no induced analogue of\nlabelledCopyCount: \"Count induced copies of a graph inside another\" is on the TODO\nlist in Mathlib's Copy.lean.\n\nIf every copy of A in B reflects adjacency, then copies of A in B are the\nsame as graph embeddings A ↪g B./-- If every copy of A in B reflects adjacency, then copies of A in B are the\nsame as graph embeddings A ↪g B. -/\ndef copyEquivEmbedding {W X : Type*} {A : SimpleGraph W} {B : SimpleGraph X}\n    (h : ∀ (f : Copy A B) {a b : W}, B.Adj (f a) (f b) → A.Adj a b) :\n    Copy A B ≃ (A ↪g B) where\n  toFun f := ⟨f.toEmbedding, ⟨h f, f.toHom.map_adj⟩⟩\n  invFun := Embedding.toCopy\n\n\nSo the labelled count of copies of Q[k] in Q[n] also counts the embeddings.\n\ntheorem card_embedding_hypercube (n k : ℕ) :\n    Nat.card (Q[k] ↪g Q[n]) = 2 ^ n * n.descFactorial k := by\n  have h := labelledCopyCount_hypercube n k\n  rwa [labelledCopyCount, Fintype.card_eq_nat_card, Nat.card_congr\n    (copyEquivEmbedding fun f _ _ => (copy_hypercube_adj_iff f).1)] at h\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\nsection\nnamespace SimpleGraph\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009Subcubes of the Hypercube*","header":"12.8.6. Every Copy is Induced","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-every-copy-is-induced"},"/Appendix/#section-chapter-11-distance-results":{"contents":"* edist_eq_hammingDist: the graph distance between any two vertices in Q[n] is\nexactly equal to their Hamming distance.* hypercube_adj_iff_hammingDist_eq_one: adjacency in Q[n] is equivalent to\nHamming distance one.* IsAcyclic.edist_eq_length: in an acyclic graph the distance between the endpoints\nof a path is its length.* edist_pathNat / ediam_pathNat / diam_pathNat: in the infinite path the\ndistance from m to m + k is k, so its diameter is ⊤ and its ℕ-valued\ndiameter is the junk value 0.* Iso.eccent_eq / Iso.ediam_eq / Iso.radius_eq: eccentricity, diameter and\nradius are isomorphism invariants.* IsVertexTransitive.eccent_eq / IsVertexTransitive.radius_eq_ediam /\nIsVertexTransitive.center_eq_univ: in a vertex-transitive graph all\neccentricities are equal, the radius equals the diameter and every vertex is\ncentral.* ediam_boxProd: the diameter of a box product of graphs on nonempty vertex types\nis the sum of the diameters.* ediam_hypercube / radius_hypercube: the hypercube Q[n] has diameter and\nradius n.* radius_mycielskian_le_two / ediam_mycielskian_le_four: the Mycielskian of a\ngraph without isolated vertices has radius at most 2 and diameter at most 4.* diam_mycielskian_le_four: the ℕ-valued diameter of any Mycielskian is at most\n4.* le_egirth_of_free: a graph containing no copy of C[n] for 3 ≤ n ≤ k has\ngirth at least k + 1.* cliqueFree_three_iff_free_cycleGraph: a graph is triangle-free iff it contains no\ncopy of C[3].* four_le_egirth_of_cliqueFree_three /\nfive_le_egirth_of_cliqueFree_three_of_free: a triangle-free graph has girth at\nleast 4, and at least 5 if it also has no 4-cycle.* egirth_cycleGraph / ediam_cycleGraph: the cycle graph C[n + 3] has girth\nn + 3 and diameter (n + 3) / 2.* egirth_completeBipartiteGraph: the complete bipartite graph K[s + 2, t + 2] has\ngirth 4.* egirth_hypercube: the girth of the hypercube Q[n + 2] is 4.* egirth_mycielskian_eq_four / egirth_mycielskian_eq_five: for triangle-free G\nthe girth of mμ[G] is 4 if two edges of G share an endpoint, and 5 if G\nhas at least one edge and its edges form a matching.* egirth_le_edist_add_edist_add_two: two distinct neighbours a and b of v,\nneither further from r than v, give a cycle of length at most\nG.edist r a + G.edist r b + 2.* moore_bound / moore_bound_of_radius_le: the Moore bound on the order of a graph\nof maximum degree at most d + 1 and diameter (or radius) at most k.* finite_of_eccent_le: a locally finite graph with a vertex of finite eccentricity\nis finite.* cage_bound_odd / cage_bound_even: the cage bounds on the order of a graph of\nminimum degree at least d + 1 and girth at least 2 * k + 1 (resp. 2 * k).* ediam_petersenGraph / egirth_petersenGraph: the Petersen graph has diameter 2\nand girth 5, so it attains the Moore bound.* Colorable.extend_precoloring: Albertson's theorem, a precolouring of vertices\npairwise at distance at least 4 in a k-colourable graph extends to a\n(k + 1)-colouring.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Results","header":"Chapter 11: Distance (Results)","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-11-distance-results"},"/Appendix/#section-chapter-4-decidability-finiteness-and-degrees":{"contents":"* myIfThenElse: branches with bif on the Boolean decide p from a Decidable p\ninstance to return a string, mirroring how ite is defined.* myClassicalIfThenElse: the same function built with classical, hence\nnoncomputable.* pathNatFromRelAbbrev: pathNatFromRel restated as an abbrev, so that Lean can\nsynthesize DecidableRel for its adjacency.* pathGraphAdjDecidableRw: deliberately flawed rw-based decidability instance,\nillustrating how a non-definitional cast blocks kernel reduction and hence\ndecide.* locallyFiniteOfFintypeEdgeSet: a graph with finitely many edges is locally\nfinite.* degIn: the number of neighbours of v lying in a finite set s, defined as the\nsize of Mathlib's s.bipartiteAbove G.Adj v.* hypercubeVertToBin: conversion of a hypercube vertex to its binary string\nrepresentation.* degreeMultiset: the multiset of degrees of all vertices in a finite graph.* degreeSequence: the list of degrees of all vertices in a finite graph, sorted in\nnon-increasing order.* Graphic: predicate for a multiset being the degree multiset of some finite simple\ngraph.* ErdosGallaiLE: the k-th Erdős–Gallai inequality for a multiset of degrees.* havelHakimiAux: the Havel–Hakimi test on a non-increasing list, merging rather\nthan re-sorting between rounds.* havelHakimi: decides whether a multiset of degrees is graphic via Havel–Hakimi.* coneGraph: adjoins a new vertex to a graph, adjacent to a chosen set of vertices.* havelHakimiEdges: constructive Havel–Hakimi, returning the edge list of a graph\nrealizing a labelled graphic degree sequence.* graphOfEdgeList: the graph on Fin n with a given list of edges, used to check\nthe output of havelHakimiEdges.\n\nFrom the exercises:\n\n* locallyFiniteBlowUpGraph: a blow-up of a locally finite graph with finite classes\nis locally finite.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"Chapter 4: Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-4-decidability-finiteness-and-degrees"},"/Distance/#section-counting-between-layers":{"contents":"Both Moore and cage bounds compare consecutive layers by counting the edges between\nthem, using two double-counting lemmas about finsets A and B of vertices. We\ncount the adjacent pairs (u, v) with u ∈ A and v ∈ B.\n\n* If every vertex of B has a neighbour in A, and every vertex of A has at most\nd neighbours in B, then #B ≤ d * #A, since each v ∈ B lies in at least one\nsuch pair and each u ∈ A in at most d.* If every vertex of A has at least d neighbours in B, and no two distinct\nvertices of A have a common neighbour in B, then d * #A ≤ #B, since now each\nu ∈ A lies in at least d pairs and each v ∈ B in at most one.\n\nBoth are instances of Mathlib's double-counting lemmas\nFinset.card_mul_le_card_mul' and Finset.card_mul_le_card_mul. We state them using\nG.degIn B u = #{v ∈ B | G.Adj u v}, the number of neighbours of u in B (see\ndegIn). In Mathlib's language these neighbours form\nB.bipartiteAbove G.Adj u, whose size is G.degIn B u by definition, and the\nneighbours of v in A form A.bipartiteBelow G.Adj v.\n\nopen Finset\n\nlemma card_le_mul_card_of_adj [DecidableRel G.Adj] {A B : Finset V} {d : ℕ}\n    (hB : ∀ v ∈ B, ∃ u ∈ A, G.Adj u v) (hA : ∀ u ∈ A, G.degIn B u ≤ d) :\n    #B ≤ d * #A := by\n  have := card_mul_le_card_mul' G.Adj (n := 1) (fun v hv => by\n    obtain ⟨u, hu, huv⟩ := hB v hv\n    exact card_pos.2 ⟨u, (mem_bipartiteBelow _).2 ⟨hu, huv⟩⟩) hA\n  grind\n\n\nlemma mul_card_le_card_of_adj [DecidableRel G.Adj] {A B : Finset V} {d : ℕ}\n    (hA : ∀ u ∈ A, d ≤ G.degIn B u)\n    (hAB : ∀ u ∈ A, ∀ x ∈ A, ∀ v ∈ B, G.Adj u v → G.Adj x v → u = x) :\n    d * #A ≤ #B := by\n  have := card_mul_le_card_mul G.Adj hA fun v hv =>\n    card_le_one.2 fun u hu x hx => by\n      rw [mem_bipartiteBelow] at hu hx\n      exact hAB u hu.1 x hx.1 v hv hu.2 hx.2\n  grind\n\n\nTo bound G.degIn B u in terms of the degree of u, we use two lemmas about\ndegIn. The Moore bound uses degIn_lt_degree_of_adj_notMem: if some neighbour of\nu lies outside B then G.degIn B u < G.degree u. The cage bounds need\ndegree_le_degIn_add_one, a bound in the other direction: if every neighbour of u,\nexcept possibly w, lies in B, then G.degree u ≤ G.degIn B u + 1.\n\n#check degIn_lt_degree_of_adj_notMem\n#check degree_le_degIn_add_one\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Moore and Cage Bounds","header":"11.4.2. Counting Between Layers","id":"/Distance/#section-counting-between-layers"}});